# Untitled

An Extraordinary Antisolvent Ethyl Cyanoformate for Achieving High Efficiency and Stability P3HT-Based CsPbI₃ Perovskite Solar Cells
Surface defects are always a severe problem in inorganic perovskite, superadding the mismatch of energy level between Poly(3-hexylthiophene) (P3HT) hole transport layer (HTL) and \(\mathsf{C s P b l}_{3}\) perovskite. All these issues result in large losses in open-circuit voltage \((V_{\mathrm{oc}})\) and fill factor \((F F)\), and thus, \(\mathsf{C s P b l}_{3}\) perovskites solar cells (PSCs) based on P3HT HTL commonly show a lower power conversion efficiency (PCE). Herein, an extraordinary antisolvent engineering is proposed by ethyl cyanoformate (EC), that regulates the crystallization progress to improve the quality of \(\mathsf{C s P b l}_{3}\) perovskite film. Concomitantly, residual EC passivates the uncoordinated \(\mathsf{P b}^{2+}\) defects by synergistical chemical interaction of \(\mathsf{C}\!\equiv\!\mathsf{N}\) and \(\mathsf{C}\!\!=\!\!\mathsf{O}\) groups, and energy level arrangement at the \(\mathsf{C s P b l}_{3}\) P3HT interface is also optimized. As a result, the PCE of P3HT-based \(\mathsf{C s P b l}_{3}\) PSC is improved to \(18.46\%\) from \(17.33\%\) of the control device. The unencapsulated \(\mathsf{C s P b l}_{3}\) PSCs show enhanced moisture stability and fine operational stability. The work reveals that EC is an extraordinary antisolvent, exhibiting a synergistic strategy in many aspects for obtaining high-performance P3HT-based \(\mathsf{C s P b l}_{3}\) PSCs. Furthermore, P3HT is doped with a small amount of 4-Cyano- \(.4^{\prime}\) -pentylbipheny (5CE), and thus the PCE of PSC is increased to \(19.15\%\) and its humidity stability is significantly improved.
## 1. Introduction
Perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have developed by leaps and bounds in a dozen years. Perovskite as a star material in the photovoltaic field is favored by more and more researchers due to their high light absorption coefficient, adjustable bandgap, low cost, and abundant reserves.[1] Up till now, the certification power conversion efficiency \((P C E)\) of organic–inorganic hybrid PSCs has
reached \(26.1\%\) ,[2] which is comparable to silicon solar cells. However, the volatility of organic cations, such as methylamine \((\mathbf{M}\mathbf{A}^{+})\) and/or formamidine \((\mathrm{FA^{+}})\) , leads to the thermal instability of organic–inorganic hybrid PSCs, and hence hinders their further commercial application.[3] Replacing organic cation with cesium cation is a promising method for solving the thermal stability of hybrid perovskite, as a result, all-inorganic PSCs emerged and received widespread attention. Among allinorganic perovskite \((\mathsf{C s P b X}_{3}\) , \(\mathrm{X}=\mathrm{Cl}\) , Br, or I), \(\mathsf{C s P b I}_{3}\) is the most promising for achieving high PCE due to the narrowest bandgap \((\approx\!1.7\ \mathrm{eV})\) .[4] To date, the \(P C E\) of \(\mathsf{C s P b I}_{3}\) PSCs with spiro-OMeTAD as hole transport layer (HTL) has boosted to the record efficiency of \(21.8\%^{[5]}\) from only \(2.9\%\) in 2015.[4b]
Despite achieving great breakthroughs in efficiency, all-inorganic PSCs still suffer from a higher loss of \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) \((\approx\!0.5\;\mathrm{V})\) than that of hybrid PSCs \((\approx\!0.3\,\mathrm{~V})^{[6]}\) because of its lower tolerance factor and softer ionic nature, which is more likely to result in defect
states at the surface and grain boundaries of perovskite during fabrication.[7] These defect states as trap centers will induce severe non-radiative recombination of carriers and then large \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) loss. To solve the problem, many strategies based on additive engineering and surface post-treatment have been reported in recent years, but few works focus on antisolvent engineering.
In the preparation of hybrid perovskite, antisolvent engineering had been considered as an effective method to obtain high-quality perovskite film by extracting the DMF/DMSO solvent with antisolvent, with which the components were quickly supersaturated and react to form black perovskite at low annealing temperature. But the process is difficult to succeed for \(\mathsf{C s P b I}_{3}\) perovskite due to its high formation energy, especially when only CsI and \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) were used as solutes, removal of DMF/DMSO by antisolvent will only result in the formation of \(\delta\) -phase perovskite, a high temperature annealing beyond \(330~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) is essential for the transformation of black-phase \(\mathsf{C s P b I}_{3}\) perovskite ( \(\alpha,\,\beta\) or \(\gamma\) phase) from \(\delta\) -phase.[8] Soon afterward, DMAI was introduced into \(\mathsf{C s P b I}_{3}\) precursor solvent to obtain black-phase \(\mathsf{C s P b I}_{3}\) at a relatively low annealing temperature of \({\approx}200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) through forming a \(\mathrm{DMAPbI}_{3}\) intermediate phase to reduce significantly the
formation energy of black-phase \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\).[9] Notably, using the DMAI-assist fabrication technology, black-phase \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) perovskite can partly formed by ester antisolvent at room temperature, however, other antisolvents, such as toluene, chlorobenzene, and diethyl ether, still failed to form black-phase \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) perovskite at the same temperature even with the assistance of DMAI.[10] Therefore, ester's antisolvent is extraordinary for the preparation of \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) perovskite. In preparation of hybrid perovskite, it had been proved that ester solvents with higher polarity can induce instant crystallization of perovskite film by more effectively remove DMSO from the freshly coated wet film without the assistant of the extra component.[11] But in the formation of black-phase \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) perovskite, DMAI seems to be indispensable. Hence, the effect of ester antisolvent on the formation of black-phase \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) perovskite should be different from that of hybrid perovskite, and the mechanism needs further exploration.
Herein, an ester solvent ethyl cyanoformate (EC) as an antisolvent was used to treat the \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) precursor film for obtaining a high-quality perovskite film. Meanwhile, we expect to figure out the intrinsic action mechanism of ester solvent and DMAI. It should also be mentioned here that a cyano terminal group \((\mathsf{C}\!\equiv\!\mathsf{N})\) is located at the terminal of the EC molecule. The \(\mathsf{C}\!\equiv\!\mathsf{N}\) was frequently used to passivate the uncoordinated \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) defect on the surface of perovskite via forming Lewis adducts.[12] Conventional antisolvent was generally used to only manipulate the crystallization process without the function of defect passivation due to their volatility. But EC has more higher boiling point than other ethyl solutions, such as ethyl acetate and methyl acetate, etc, and thus is expected to further passivate the defects during antisolvent treatment by coordination between \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) and \(\mathsf{C}\!\equiv\!\mathsf{N}\) group. As the results, it is found that EC improves the quality of \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) perovskite through the following mechanism: in the beginning, EC can act as antisolvent to rapidly remove DMF/DMSO, promoting the formation of \(\mathrm{DMAPbI}_{3}\) intermediate phase; Subsequently, EC redissolved of DMAI in \(\mathrm{DMAPbI}_{3}\) intermediate phase, the released \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) (i.e., \(\mathrm{PbI}_{6}\) octahedron) easily react with \(\mathrm{Cs}^{+}\)and then black \(\gamma\mathrm{-CsPbI}_{3}\) perovskite began to nucleate and grow. Based on this, the rate of crystallization of perovskite during subsequent annealing was increased, and the morphology of perovskite was improved. After annealing, residual EC molecular synergistically passivate the uncoordinated \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) defect in perovskite through the formation of \(\mathrm{Pb-O}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb-N}\) coordination bonds. In addition, the energy band of obtained \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) perovskite up-shifted, and energy level alignment between \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) perovskite and P3HT became more matched. As a result, the \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) and \(E F\) were significantly increased from 1.11 to \(1.14\,\mathrm{V}\) and from \(77.14\%\) to \(79.96\%\) , respectively, and hence the \(P C E\) was greatly improved from \(17.33\%\) to \(18.46\%\) . Due to the improvement of the quality of perovskite film and defect passivation, the unencapsulated \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) PSCs with EC-treatment also showed the improvement of moisture stability with maintaining \(71\%\) of initial \(P C E\) under \(25{-}35\%\) RH in air for \(200\;\mathrm{h}\) . Moreover, the PSC also presented fine operational stability and maintained \({\approx}90\%\) of its initial \(P C E\) over \(170\,\mathrm{h}\) of irradiation. Furthermore, we also doped P3HT with a small amount of 4-Cyano- \(.4^{\prime}\) -pentylbiphenyl (5CB) according to our previous work,[13] further increased the \(P C E\) of PSC to \(19.15\%\) , and significantly improved its humidity stability.
## 2. Results and Discussion
Figure 1a shows the fabrication process of \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) film with EC-treatment. The chemical structure of the EC molecule is shown in Figure S1 (Supporting Information), in which \(\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{O}\) , \(-\mathrm{O}-\) , and \(\mathsf{C}\!\equiv\!\mathsf{N}\) groups are expected to form the chemical interaction with undercoordinated \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) in the perovskite film to reduce defects due to the lone pair of electrons in \(\mathrm{O}\) and \(_\mathrm{N}\) atoms. As we can see in Figure 1b, after EC drip coating, the color of the perovskite precursor wet film immediately changed to light brown. In the subsequent annealing process, the EC-treated film exhibited a faster speed of turning to the black phase than the control film, reflecting the accelerated nucleation and crystallization progress. In general, the rapid crystallization and growth of perovskite can avoid excessive contact with oxygen and water during annealing, which is conducive to improving the quality of \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) perovskite film.[9a] This point was confirmed by the result of the XRD pattern, as shown in Figure 1c. The two diffraction peaks at \(14.3^{\circ}\) and \(28.9^{\circ}\) corresponding to (110) and (220) crystal plane of \(\gamma\mathrm{-CsPbI}_{3}\) perovskite,[14] respectively, showed stronger intensity when perovskite film was treated by EC, suggesting that the crystal quality of perovskite was improved. Furthermore, a little diffraction peak at \(11.6^{\circ}\) , which is assigned to the \(\mathrm{DMAPbI}_{3}\) intermediate phase (see Figure S2, Supporting Information), was detected in the control \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) film. The existence of \(\mathrm{DMAPbI}_{3}\) in the control film reflected that the conventional annealing process cannot guarantee the complete transformation of \(\mathrm{DMAPbI}_{3}\) to \(\gamma\mathrm{-CsPbI}_{3}\) , while the residual \(\mathrm{DMAPbI}_{3}\) intermediate phase is harmful to the photovoltaic performance of \(\mathrm{PSCs}\).[14] Fortunately, the \(\mathrm{DMAPbI}_{3}\) was completely transformed to \(\gamma\mathrm{-CsPbI}_{3}\) in the perovskite with EC-treatment. Therefore, the UV–vis absorption intensity also increased slightly for the EC-treated perovskite as well (Figure 1d). In addition, both XRD peaks and absorption edge remain unchanged (Figure S3, Supporting Information), meaning that EC-treatment did not change the lattice structure and bandgap of \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) perovskite.
Surface morphology was also measured through atomic force microscopy (AFM) images, see Figure 1e,f. The root-mean-square-roughness (Rq) of EC-treated film decreased to \(3.03\ \mathrm{nm}\) from pristine of \(5.27\ \mathrm{nm}\) , proving that a smoother \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) film was obtained through EC-treatment. Figure \(^{1}\mathrm{g-j}\) shows the top-view and cross-sectional SEM images of perovskite films with and without EC-treatment. It is observed that there are many pinholes on the surface of the control film, while the incorporation of EC enables the formation of the extremely uniform and dense film, thus the surface of EC-treated perovskite film became smoother than that of the control, which is consistent with AFM results. Judging from the cross-sectional images, we found that there were many grains with large size differences in the control film, while the grain size became relatively uniform in EC EC-treated film, it may be related to the crystallization rate of perovskite.
Immediate darkening of perovskite wet film induced by EC drop coating indicates that EC accelerated the nucleation and crystallization of \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) perovskite, and thus the black phase \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) formed earlier compared to the control film. In order to find out if the role of EC used here is the same as that of conventional antisolvents in organic–inorganic hybrid PSCs, chlorobenzene (CB) was also applied to treat wet precursor film. Figure 2a,b show the photographs and corresponding X-ray
diffraction (XRD) patterns of perovskite precursor films treated by different solvents. As we can see in Figure 2b, no diffraction peak was found in the freshly finished precursor film without antisolvent treatment, suggesting that excessive DMF/DMSO in the film make precursor film incapable of crystallization. When the film was preheated at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(1\,\mathrm{min}\), two diffraction peaks \(\approx\!11.6^{\circ}\) and \(12.1^{\circ}\) appeared though the color was still yellow. The two peaks should correspond to \(\mathrm{DMAPbI}_{3}\) intermediate phase and \(\mathrm{Cs}_{4}\mathrm{PbI}_{6}\),[9c,15] respectively, that is, the preheated precursor film is composed of \(\mathrm{DMAPbI}_{3}\) and \(\mathrm{Cs}_{4}\mathrm{PbI}_{6}\). According to the previous report,[9c,15] \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) tends to preferentially react with DMAI and then form \(\mathrm{DMAPbI}_{3}\) intermediate phase accompanied with a small amount of \(\mathrm{Cs}_{4}\mathrm{PbI}_{6}\) phase, rather than non-perovskite \(\delta\)-phase \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) during annealing. Subsequently, the two kinds of phase of \(\mathrm{DMAPbI}_{3}\) and \(\mathrm{Cs}_{4}\mathrm{PbI}_{6}\) transform to black \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) phase. The peak \(\approx\!25.5^{\circ}\) should be assigned to \(\mathrm{CsI-PbI}_{2}\) –DMSO adduct similar to \(\mathrm{MAI-PbI}_{2}\) –DMSO previously reported,[8] this may be due to the short annealing time and insufficient removal of DMSO.
For CB treated precursor film, owing to the low polarity of CB limited its ability to remove DMSO, hence the peak of \(\mathrm{CsI-PbI}_{2}\) – DMSO adduct still existed. More importantly, the color remains the same light yellow as that of the control film without antisolvent treatment, but its XRD pattern showed stronger diffraction peaks of the \(\mathrm{DMAPbI}_{3}\) phase, verifying that CB acts as antisolvent only extract excessive DMF/DMSO to promote the reaction between DMAI and \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\), cannot induce the formation of black phase \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\). UV–vis spectrum of the CB treated precursor film also exhibited the same result as the control film (Figure 2c). But for EC treated precursor film, a completely different phenomenon was observed. First, the disappearance of \(\mathrm{CsI-PbI}_{2}\) – DMSO adduct confirmed the strong polarity of EC, which could effectively remove DMSO. Then visibly, the film color changed to light brown, and its XRD pattern showed obvious diffraction peaks of the \(\gamma\mathrm{-CsPbI}_{3}\) phase at \(14.3^{\circ}\) and \(28.9^{\circ}\). UV–vis spectrum of EC treated precursor film also exhibited a distinct absorption edge \(\approx\!730\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Figure 2c), revealing that \(\gamma\mathrm{-CsPbI}_{3}\) perovskite crystal had formed in EC treated precursor film. Furthermore, a small amount of \(\mathrm{DMAPbI}_{3}\) phase was also detected in EC treated precursor film. These results prove that EC plays a more complex role than CB as an antisolvent in the formation process of \(\gamma\mathrm{-CsPbI}_{3}\) perovskite. The possible mechanism is that EC promotes the transform from \(\mathrm{DMAPbI}_{3}\) to \(\gamma\mathrm{-CsPbI}_{3}\), hence a significantly reduced intensity of \(\mathrm{DMAPbI}_{3}\) diffraction peak was observed in EC treated precursor film compared to that of CB treated film. As for the specific reaction principle, we surmised that EC could dissolve \(\mathrm{DMA^{+}}\) of \(\mathrm{DMAPbI}_{3}\), and then \(\mathrm{Cs^{+}}\) occupies the position of \(\mathrm{DMA^{+}}\) in the original lattice of \(\mathrm{DMAPbI}_{3}\) and thus promotes the formation of \(\gamma\mathrm{-CsPbI}_{3}\).
To test this hypothesis, another conventional antisolvent isopropanol (IPA) with strong polarity was used as a comparison, because it had been reported that alcohol solvent could extract organic ammonium salt of hybrid perovskite with different dimensions.[15] As we can see in Figure 2a–c, the precursor film with IPA treatment showed a darker color and possessed the stronger XRD peaks of \(\gamma\mathrm{-CsPbI}_{3}\) and the higher intensity at light absorption edge than those of the film with EC- treatment, representing more \(\gamma\mathrm{-CsPbI}_{3}\) formed, but no \(\mathrm{DMAPbI}_{3}\) phase was detected in IPA treated film (see Figure 2b). If IPA is similar to
CB as a conventional antisolvent, the XRD signal of \(\mathrm{DMAPbI}_{3}\) intermediate phase should present in IPA treated film, but this speculation departure from the facts. In addition, it is also impossible for IPA treatment to directly form black perovskite because when we remove DMAI from the perovskite precursor solution, IPA acts as an antisolvent to promote the formation of \(\gamma\mathrm{-CsPbI}_{3}\) (see Figure S4, Supporting Information). Because alcohols solvent could extract organic ammonium salt from hybrid perovskite with different dimensions,[15] therefore, the phenomenon that happened in IPA-treated film can be explained as follows: \(\mathrm{DMAPbI}_{3}\) intermediate phase was formed initially owing to the antisolvent effect, then completely transformed to black phase perovskite \(\gamma\mathrm{-CsPbI}_{3}\) through quickly extracting the organic ammonium salt of DMAI by IPA. Figure S4 (Supporting Information) also shows that the precursor film (prepared with only \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CsI}\) as solutes) treated by EC still remain yellow, and only \(\delta\mathrm{-CsPbI}_{3}\) was detected in the corresponding XRD pattern, revealing that EC cannot directly promote the formation of black phase \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) perovskite. So, we deduced that EC could perform a similar way to IPA to form \(\gamma\mathrm{-CsPbI}_{3}\), but the produced \(\mathrm{DMAPbI}_{3}\) by antisolvent effect only partly transformed to \(\gamma\mathrm{-CsPbI}_{3}\) due to the lower solubility of DMAI in EC than in IPA (see Figure S5, Supporting Information, there were many undissolved solute particles on the wall of a bottle of \(20\,\mathrm{mg\,mL^{-1}}\) in EC, the corresponding dynamic light scattering (DLS) measurement also revealed the lower solubility of DMAI in EC than in IPA), hence the signal of \(\mathrm{DMAPbI}_{3}\) still presented in EC treated film and the diffraction peak intensity of \(\gamma\mathrm{-CsPbI}_{3}\) in EC treated film is relatively lower than that in IPA treated film. Moreover, although there was more \(\gamma\mathrm{-CsPbI}_{3}\) perovskite crystal formed in IPA-treated precursor film, the morphology of the IPA-treated film was not better than that of the EC-treated film, as shown in Figure S6 (Supporting Information). The possible reason is that the terminal group of \(-\mathrm{OH}\) (with too strong polarity) in IPA would bind easily to water molecules during the film annealing in the air, which would erode the surface of perovskite, so many pinholes presented in the surface of IPA-treated perovskite film.
Based on the above analysis, we put forward the possible reaction mechanism that happened in EC-treating film, the reaction formulas are shown below:
\[
\mathrm{DMAI}+\mathrm{DMF}/\mathrm{DMSO}+\mathrm{PbI}_2\xrightarrow{EC}\mathrm{DMAPbI}_2+\mathrm{DMF}/\mathrm{DMSO}\uparrow
\]
\[
\mathrm{DMPbI}_3\xrightarrow{EC}\mathrm{DMAI}+\mathrm{PbI}_2
\]
\[
\mathrm{PbI}_2+\mathrm{CsI}\xrightarrow{DMAI}\gamma-\mathrm{CsPbI}_3
\]
Among them, Formula 1 expressed the antisolvent effect of EC, Formulas 2 and 3 expressed the process of EC promoting the formation of black phase \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) perovskite. The corresponding mechanism diagram was also drawn in Figure 3a. Combining the reaction formulas, we proposed the functional mechanism of EC as follow: EC first act as antisolvent to extract DMF/DMSO to facilitate the generation of \(\mathrm{DMAPbI}_{3}\) intermediate phase, part of which would be decomposed subsequently by EC through extracting \(\mathrm{DMA^{+}}\), meantime, the released \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) would soon react with \(\mathrm{Cs^{+}}\) to form \(\gamma\mathrm{-CsPbI}_{3}\) nuclei with assistance of DMAI.[15] According to the nucleation-growth theory, once the size of
There is no report about the defect passivation effect of conventional antisolvents because these antisolvents tend to volatilize and escape from the resulting perovskite during annealing. Whether EC molecules remain in the annealed films and the defects can be passivated by EC needs to be verified. Therefore, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurement was carried out. As shown in Figure 4a, the signal of N 1s orbit can be detected in the annealed film, reflecting that there were still residual EC molecules in the finished perovskite film. More remarkably, for the perovskite film after being treated by EC, Pb 4f peaks shift toward lower binding energy and N 1s peak shift toward higher binding energy (Figure 4c), with unchanged in Cs 3d, I 3d or bits (Figure S7, Supporting Information), meaning the obvious chemical interaction between \(\mathrm{C}{\equiv}\mathrm{N}\) group of EC and undercoordinated \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) on the perovskite surface. In addition, O 1s orbital with asymmetric broad peaks were deconvoluted into two subpeaks. One peak at a high binding energy location is assigned to \(-\mathrm{O}-\) bond, and another peak at a low binding energy location is assigned \(\mathrm{C}{=}\mathrm{O}\) bond. As we can see in Figure 4b, the O 1s peaks belonging to \(-\mathrm{O}-\) and \(\mathrm{C}{=}\mathrm{O}\) bonds shifted to the larger binding energy with the shift of 0.54 and \(0.7\ \mathrm{eV}\), respectively, revealing that the O atoms in \(\mathrm{C}{=}\mathrm{O}\) and \(-\mathrm{O}-\) groups also had a strong chemical interaction with undercoordinated \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) and thus confirming the defect passivating defects of EC. But it was observed that the shift distance of O 1s peaks was still smaller than that of the N 1s peak \((0.85\;\mathrm{eV})\). This result reflected that the N atom showed a stronger interaction with perovskite than the O atom. Furthermore, obviously disappeared \(\mathrm{Pb^{0}}\) peak after EC treatment also proved that EC can effectively passivate the metallic Pb defects in perovskite.
