Density functional theory (DFT) calculations were also carried to help us better understand the passivation effect of EC on the defects of perovskite. We built a \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) cluster model with exposed \(\mathrm{Pb}\) ions to simulate the interaction between EC and perovskite. We simulated several docking positions of EC on the exposed \(\mathrm{Pb}\) surface, as shown in Figure S8 (Supporting Information), it is clearly that the individual N \((\mathrm{C}{\equiv}\mathrm{N})\), O \((\mathrm{C}{=}\mathrm{O}\) and \(-\mathrm{O}-)\) would form stable N—Pb and O—Pb bonds with exposed \(\mathrm{Pb}\) after geometry optimizations, the formation energy of three motifs are \(-2.34\), \(-2.20\), and \(-2.14\,\mathrm{eV}\), respectively. Besides the above three motifs, we found that N of \(\mathrm{C}{\equiv}\mathrm{N}\) and O of \(\mathrm{C}{=}\mathrm{O}\) would simultaneously form N—Pb and O—Pb with two neighboring \(\mathrm{Pb}\), this motif showed the highest formation energy of \(-2.36\,\mathrm{eV}\). To sum up, the DFT results suggested that EC could theoretically passivate surface defects through interaction between N, O, and \(\mathrm{Pb}\). Among the various interactions, the individual action of \(\mathrm{C}{\equiv}\mathrm{N}\) and
the synergistic action effect of \(\mathsf{C}\!\equiv\!\mathsf{N}\) and \(\scriptstyle{C=0}\) with undercoordinated \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) were more effective to passivate the surface defects of perovskite, which is consistent with XPS results. The above investigations indicate that EC was unusual compared to the conventional antisolvents, playing not only the antisolvent effect to improve the crystallization quality of perovskite but also the defect passivation effect.
Except for XPS evidence for the defect passivation effect of EC on perovskite, the suppression of ion migration is another proof to reduce defect density. To evaluate the effect of EC on ion migration in perovskite films, the temperature-dependent conductivity of perovskite was tested based on the device with the structure of glass/perovskite (without or with EC treating)/Ag. The activation energy \(\left(E_{\mathrm{a}}\right)\) can be calculated according to the Nernst–Einstein equation, \(\sigma(T)=\left(\sigma_{0}/T\right)\exp(-E_{\mathrm{a}}/k_{\mathrm{B}}T)\), where \(\sigma_{0}\) is a constant, \(k_{\mathrm{B}}\) is Boltzmann's constant, \(\sigma\) is the ionic conductivity, \(T\) is the temperature, and \(E_{\mathrm{a}}\) is the activation energy. As shown in Figure 4d, the \(E_{\mathrm{a}}\) of EC-treated perovskite is significantly increased to 0.078 from \(0.044\,\mathrm{eV}\) of the control, indicating an effective suppression of ion migration in perovskite film, which is beneficial to reduce the possibility of defect formation. The suppression of ion migration was also confirmed by the element content ratio between I and Pb (Table S1, Supporting Information), which is extracted from energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) measurement. As shown in Figure 4e, a lot of I vacancy defects would be induced into perovskite film owing to the migration of I ion, hence making the ratio of I:Pb far below 3, with the value of only 2.82. After treating with EC, this ratio increased to 3.01, almost close to the standard value of 3, further revealing the effective suppression of ion migration. Moreover, the lower ratio of Cs:Pb (0.82) in the control film proves \(\mathrm{DMAPbI}_{3}\) intermediate phase still exists in perovskite, while in EC treated sample, the ratio of Cs:Pb increased to 0.90, certifying EC-treatment is beneficial for transformation of \(\mathrm{DMAPbI}_{3}\) intermediate phase.
Then, the steady-state photoluminescence (PL) and transient photoluminescence (TRPL) were applied to characterize the defect density-related photophysics properties of different \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) perovskite films deposited on bare glass. As shown in Figure 4f, EC treated sample presented a stronger intensity of fluorescence than the control film, illustrating that defects-assistant nonradiative recombination loss was suppressed by EC. Meantime, the slight blue-shift of the PL peak in the EC treated sample also reflected the elimination of shallow sub-bandgap defects. TRPL measurements were then carried out to explore carrier transfer kinetics. The TRPL spectra are shown in Figure 4g and the detailed lifetime parameters extracted by bi-exponential fitting methods are shown in Table S2 (Supporting Information). Clearly, the average lifetime \(\left(\tau_{\mathrm{ave}}\right)\) of the control film is 92.39 ns, and then sharply prolongs to 155.66 ns after EC-treatment. The increased \(\tau_{\mathrm{ave}}\) is in keeping with the fluorescence of PL intensity, indicating the longer lifetime of carriers due to the reduced defect density.
In the end, the space-charge-limited-current (SCLC) test based on a hole-only device with the structure of FTO/PEDOT:PSS/PVK/P3HT/Ag was conducted. The defects density \(\left(N_{\mathrm{trap}}\right)\) can be quantitatively calculated according to the equation of \(N_{\mathrm{trap}}=\left(2V_{\mathrm{PFL}}\tau_{\mathrm{avg}}\right)/\left(\sigma L^{2}\right)\), where \(V_{\mathrm{PLF}}\) is the trap-filled limit voltage, \(\epsilon\) is the relative dielectric constant of \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) perovskite, \(\epsilon_{0}\) is the vacuum dielectric constant, \(\epsilon\) is the charge of an electron, and \(L\) is the thickness of the \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) perovskite film.[16] The typical dark \(J{-}V\) curves of the devices are shown in Figure 4h, the \(V_{\mathrm{TFL}}\) of control and EC treating-based devices are 0.29 and \(0.15\,\mathrm{~V~}\), respectively. In consequence, the calculated defect density for EC treated sample reduced from \(2.25\times10^{15}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}\) of the control to \(1.16\times10^{15}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}\), the lower defect density reduced the possibility of carrier capture at defect-related recombination center, which is of great significance to the promotion of \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\).
Whether the energy level of perovskite matches with that of the hole transport layer is also a crucial factor to affects the performance of PSCs. Therefore, the ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) of \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) perovskite without or with EC-treatment was measured to evaluate the energy level alignment, as shown in Figure 5a. The work function (WF) can be determined from the cutoff energy \(\left(E_{\mathrm{cutoff}}\right)\) (see left picture of Figure 5a) of UPS spectra by the expression \(\mathrm{WF} = 21.22\mathrm{eV} - E_{\mathrm{cutoff}}\). The intersection of the tangent lines of the UPS curve at the Fermi edge (see the right picture of Figure 5a) is the distance between WF and conduction band minimum (CBM). Optical bandgaps of different perovskite films were extracted from the Tauc plot (Figure S3, Supporting Information), which was conversed from UV–vis spectrum (Figure 1d). The calculated band location of different perovskite was presented in Figure S9 (Supporting Information), it can be found that the WF of EC treated sample shift up from \(-4.28\) to \(-4.17\ \mathrm{eV}\), then since the Fermi edge is almost constant, the CBM of perovskite shift to higher position of \(-5.41\) from \(-5.30\ \mathrm{eV}\). It is distinct that \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) perovskite after EC-treating brought a favorable energy level alignment at the \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}/\mathrm{HTL}\) interface, and charge separation and transfer from \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) to HTL would be accelerated and thus reduce interfacial charge recombination loss. PL and TRPL measurements with HTL coated further confirmed the improvement of hole transport. As shown in Figure 5b,c, the EC-treated sample showed obvious fluorescence quenching effect compared to control sample, and corresponding TRPL spectra also exhibited a shorter \(\tau_{\mathrm{ave}}\) of \(8.04\ \mathrm{ns}\), while \(\tau_{\mathrm{ave}}\) of the control sample is \(10.88\ \mathrm{ns}\) (detailed lifetime parameters extracted by bi-exponential fitting methods are shown in Table S3, Supporting Information).
Then, the typical planar \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) PSCs with an \(n{-}i{-}p\) structure of \(\mathrm{FTO/TiO}_{2}/\ \mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}/\mathrm{HTL/Ag}\) were prepared to evaluate their performance after EC-treatment. At the first, the optimized energy level alignment is beneficial to the improvement of \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\), and \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) is directly related to built-in electric
field of the \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) PSCs. Therefore, we performed capacitance-voltage measurement to clarify the change of the built-in electric field, which is obtained from the Mott–Schottky equation of \(C^{-2} = (2(V_{\mathrm{bi}} - V))/(A^{2}e\epsilon\epsilon_{0}N_{\mathrm{A}})\), where \(C\) is the capacitance under applied voltage \(V\), \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) is the built-in potential, \(A\) is the device area, \(\epsilon\) is the relative dielectric constant of \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) perovskite, \(\epsilon_{0}\) is the vacuum dielectric constant, \(\epsilon\) is the charge of an electron, and \(N_{\mathrm{A}}\) is carrier concentration.[17] As shown in Figure 5d, benefiting from the better-matched energy level between \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) perovskite and P3HT, EC-treated PSCs yield a higher \(V_{\mathrm{bi}}\) of \(0.91\ \mathrm{V}\) than control PSCs \((0.88\ \mathrm{V})\). The enhancement in \(V_{\mathrm{bi}}\) would provide a stronger driving force to separate the photogenerated carriers, simultaneously, the depletion region would be extended to suppress electron-hole recombination at the \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}/\mathrm{HTL}\) interface, ultimately gives rise to higher \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) and better photovoltaic performance of PSCs.
The electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) analysis was also applied to characterize the charge transport and recombination properties of different \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) PSCs. The EIS tests were carried out at an applied bias of \(0\ \mathrm{V}\) under dark conditions, corresponding Nyquist plot is shown in Figure 5e, the parameters of series resistance \(\left(R_{\mathrm{sc}}\right)\), charge transport resistance \(\left(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\right)\), and recombination resistance \(\left(R_{\mathrm{rec}}\right)\) are listed in Table S4 (Supporting Information), all those parameters were extracted by fitting the curves using equivalent circuit, which was inserted in Figure 5f. Among them, \(R_{\mathrm{s}}\), representing the film resistance of the entire device, showed a slight decrease from \(64.37\) to \(54.09\ \Omega\) after EC-treatment, owing to closer contact between perovskite and HTL caused by the smoother surface of perovskite. As well, \(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\) reduced from \(0.29\) to \(0.24\ \mathrm{M}\Omega\), indicating a promoted charge transfer due to better matched energy level at \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}/\mathrm{P3HT}\) interface. And in agreement with the results of \(N_{\mathrm{trap}}\) value, the device based on EC-treatment presents a larger \(R_{\mathrm{rec}}\) of \(7.09\ \mathrm{M}\Omega\) than that of the control device \((4.21\ \mathrm{M}\Omega)\), illustrating the suppressed trap-assistance recombination.
The defects in perovskite were also characterized by thermal admittance spectroscopy through calculating the capacitance–frequency (C–f) spectrum of PSCs. As shown in Figure 5f, the trap density of states (tDOS) of the EC-treated PSC significantly reduced compared with the control PSC in the region below \( 0.35~\mathrm{eV} \). The reduced trap states further confirmed the efficient passivation role of EC.[18] In addition, the charge recombination and transport dynamics were confirmed by the transient photovoltage decay (TPV) and transient photocurrent decay (TPC), respectively (see Figure 5g,h). The TPV decay curve of EC-treated PSCs showed a much longer lifetime \( (1027\,\upmu\mathrm{s}) \) than control PSCs \( (340~\upmu\mathrm{s}) \), indicating a significant decrease of charge recombination. Meanwhile, the TPV response of EC-treated PSC was quicker than that of control PSC, proving the faster charge transport in the PSCs with EC-treatment.
Subsequently, the photovoltaic performance of devices was evaluated. The box diagram of PCE based on 40 devices for each kind are shown in Figure 6a (the box diagrams of other parameters were shown in Figure S10, Supporting Information), and relational photovoltaic parameters were listed in Table S5 (Supporting Information). The \( J{-}V \) curves and detailed photovoltaic parameters extracted from the best device made with and without EC- treatment are shown in Figure 6b . The control \( \mathrm{CsPbI}_{3} \) PSC showed an average \( P C E \) of \( 16.90\% \) with an average \( V_{\mathrm{oc}} \) of \( 1.10\;\mathrm{V}, \) an average \( J_{\mathrm{sc}} \) of \( 20.26~\mathrm{mA~cm}^{-2} \), and an average \( F F \) of 75.97. while the EC-treated PSC exhibits a remarkably enhanced average \( P C E \) of \( 18.03\% \) with an average \( V_{\mathrm{oc}} \) of \( 1.13\,\mathrm{V}, \) an average \( J_{\mathrm{sc}} \) of \( 20.25~\mathrm{mA~cm}^{-2} \), and an average \( F F \) of 79.09. It is visible that the enhanced performance was mainly related to the improvement
in \( V_{\mathrm{oc}} \) and \( F F \), which should be credited to suppressed defect-related nonradiative recombination loss and optimized energy level arrangement. Eventually, the champion EC-treated device was obtained with the highest \( P C E \) of \( 18.46\% \), a \( V_{\mathrm{oc}} \) of \( 1.14\;\mathrm{V}, \) a \( J_{\mathrm{sc}} \) of \( 20.24\;\mathrm{mA\;cm}^{-2} \) and an \( F F \) of 79.96.
Figure S11 (Supporting Information) showed the external quantum efficiency (EQE) curves based on different PSCs. The \( J_{\mathrm{sc}} \) value from EQE spectra of devices made with and without EC-treatment were 19.45 and \( 19.41\;\mathrm{mA\;cm}^{-2} \), respectively. Both integrated \( J_{\mathrm{sc}} \) are within a \( 5\% \) deviation from \( J_{\mathrm{sc}} \) obtained by \( J{-}V \) measurement, verifying the accuracy of \( J{-}V \) measurement. Then the steady power output (SPO) of the best EC-treated device was pre-formed at the maximum power output point with an external bias at \( 0.976\;\mathrm{V} \) (Figure 6c), the PCE kept at \( 18.01\% \) when tested for \( 400\;s \), with the corresponding steady \( J_{\mathrm{sc}} \) of \( 18.45\;\mathrm{mA\;cm}^{-2} \). In addition, the hysteresis index \( (H I) \), which can be estimated through the equation of \( H I = (P C E_{\mathrm{reverse}} - P C E_{\mathrm{forward}}) / P C E_{\mathrm{reverse}} \), where \( P C E_{\mathrm{reverse}} \) and \( P C E_{\mathrm{forward}} \) are the \( P C E \) of PSCs obtained from reverse and forward scanned \( J{-}V \) curves, respectively. Corresponding photovoltaic parameters are listed in Table S4 (Supporting Information), the \( H I \) of EC treated device obviously decreased to \( 2.50\% \) from pristine \( 3.73\% \) (Figure S12, Supporting Information), which could be attributed to the comprehensive improvement of reduced defect density and optimized energy level alignment.
Then we investigated the humidity stability of different \( \mathrm{CsPbI}_{3} \) PSCs. The water contact angle was firstly measured, as shown in Figure S13, (Supporting Information), the angle of EC treated sample increased a bit from \( 89.7^{\circ} \) to \( 104.2^{\circ} \), in consideration
of the absent hydrophobic group in EC molecular except for an ethyl group, the increased in water contact angle may come from the improvement of perovskite crystallization quality and defects passivation. Perovskite films and unencapsulated devices were stored in ambient air at a relative humidity range of \(25{-}35\%\). The picture of the evolution process of \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) films is presented in Figure S14 (Supporting Information). Consistent with the water contact angle, EC-treated film shows a relatively slow phase transition process from black phase to yellow non-perovskite phase. And the corresponding devices without encapsulation similar decay trend in PCE, as shown in Figure 6d, where the EC-treated device remained at \(71\%\) of its PCE after exposing to the air for \(200\,\mathrm{h}\), and the PCE of the control device was only \(39\%\) of its initial value in the same period. All these results reflected the improved humidity stability of the film and device with EC-treatment. Then we compared the thermal stability of perovskite films and PSCs at \(80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) atmosphere. As shown in Figure S15 (Supporting Information), the color of the films remains black after \(120\,\mathrm{h}\) of annealing, and both two kinds of PSCs show essentially the same decline tendency in efficiency. The results confirmed the natural good thermal stability of \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) perovskite, and EC has little effect on its thermal stability. We also tested the operational stability of the unencapsulated devices under continuous light irradiation \((100\mathrm{\mW\cm}^{2}\) without UV-cutting) in \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\). The PSC of EC treated sample presented fine operational stability and maintained \({\approx}90\%\) of its initial PCE over \(170\,\mathrm{h}\) of irradiation (Figure 6e).
Furthermore, as our previous report,[13] 4-Cyano-4-Pentylbiphenyl (5CB) played an excellent role in improving the hole mobility and regulating the energy level arrangement of P3HT, therefore, we dropped P3HT with 4-Cyano-4-Pentylbiphenyl, as the result, the \(P C E\) of \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) PSCs was further improved to \(19.15\%\) (see Figure 6f). We also studied the stability of \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) film and PSC based on the synergistic effect of EC treating and 5CB doping. Because 5CB is doped in P3HT solution, so the film used for stability measurement is the perovskite film coated with P3HT. The results are shown in Figure S16 (Supporting Information). It is observed that the films based on EC treating or \(\mathrm{EC}/5\mathrm{CB}\) synergistically treating both show negligible changes when placed on a hotplate at \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(120\,\mathrm{~h~}\) in \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) atmosphere, and the PCE of corresponding devices both maintain \({\approx}90\%\) of their initial PCE after annealing for \(240~\mathrm{h}\). In terms of humidity stability, the film based on only EC treating completely transform to yellow \(\delta\) phase when the film is placed in \(45\,\pm\,5\%\) RH air for \(120\,\mathrm{~h~}\), the PCE of the corresponding unencapsulated device rapidly decreased to only \(11\%\) of its initial value after staying in moist air for \(240~\mathrm{h}\). By contrast, when synergistically modified with EC and 5CB, the film exhibits a significantly slower phase transition rate, and the corresponding device shows a stronger humidity stability with remaining almost \(55\%\) of initial PCE under the same storage condition. This is due to the hydrophobicity of the benzene ring and alkyl chain on the both sides of 5CB molecule, which has been described in our previous report.[13] Finally, we tested the operational stability of the unencapsulated devices under continuous light irradiation \((100\,\mathrm{mW\,cm}^{2}\) without UV-cut) in an \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) atmosphere. As we can see, these two kinds of PSCs present a similar decay trend by remaining \({\approx}90\%\) of their initial PCE over \(170\,\mathrm{h}\) of irradiation, exhibiting a T80 lifetime (i.e., the time
it takes for PCE to degrade to \(80\%\) of its initial value) \({\approx}350\,\mathrm{h}\) by linearly extrapolation. In summary, as with the PSCs based on only EC-treatment, the PSCs based on \(\mathrm{EC}/5\mathrm{CB}\) synergistically treatment also present a fine operational stability.
## 3. Conclusion
In this study, an ethyl cyanoformate (EC) antisolvent was used to treat the \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) perovskite precursor film. EC accelerated the crystallization progress of perovskite and improved its quality. After annealing, residual EC molecular synergistically passivates uncoordinated \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) defect in perovskite through the formation \(\mathrm{Pb}-\mathrm{O}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb}-\mathrm{N}\) coordination bonds. In addition, the energy level alignment between \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) perovskite and P3HT was optimized by the addition of EC. As the result, the \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) and \(F F\) were significantly increased, and hence the PCE was greatly improved from \(17.33\%\) to \(18.46\%\). Due to the improvement of the quality of perovskite film and defect passivation, the unencapsulated \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) PSCs with EC-treatment also show improved moisture stability and maintained \(71\%\) of initial PCE under \(25{-}35\%\) RH in air for \(200\,\mathrm{h}\). Moreover, the PSC also presented fine operational stability and maintained \({\approx}90\%\) of its initial PCE over \(170\,\mathrm{h}\) of irradiation. Subsequently, according to our previous report, we doped P3HT with a small amount of 5CB, further increased the PCE of PSC to \(19.15\%\), and significantly improved its humidity stability.
## 4. Experimental Section
Materials: Ti (IV) bis (ethyl acetoacetate)-diisopropoxide was obtained from Sigma–Aldrich. The cesium iodide (CsI; \(99.99\%\)) and DMAI were purchased from Xi'an Polymer Light Technology Corporation. P3HT (\(>\!90\%\) regio-regularity, molecular weight \({\approx}6000\mathrm{kDA}\)) was purchased from 1-Material INC. N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF; \(99.8\%\)), Dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO; \(99.9\%\)) chlorobenzene (CB; \(99.8\%\)) and ethyl cyanoformate (EC) were purchased from Aldrich.
Device Fabrication: The \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) PSCs were fabricated with a structure of \(\mathrm{FTO/TiO_{2}/C s P b I_{3}/P3H T/A g}\). FTO glass substrates were cleaned with distilled water, acetone, and isopropanol in an ultrasonic bath for \(15\;\mathrm{min}\), respectively. \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) films as the electron transport layer (ETL) were prepared by spin-coating a \(0.2\;\mathsf{M}\) Ti (IV) bis (ethyl acetoacetate)-diisopropoxide solution in 1-butanol on FTO glass at \(4000\ \mathsf{r p m}\) for \(30~\mathsf{s}\), and then being annealed at \(450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in muffle furnace for \(60\;\mathsf{m i n}\) with a \(2~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}~\mathsf{s}^{-1}\) heating rate. The \(0.7\,\mathsf{\,M\,\,C s P b l}_{3}\) perovskite precursor was prepared by dissolving \(0.182\;\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{C}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{I}\) , \(0.1211\;\mathrm{g}\) DMAI and \(0.3227\;\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) in \(1~\mathsf{m L}\) DMF/DMSO solvent (volume ratio is 4:1), then the \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) precursor were spin-coated on \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) film at \(3000\;\mathsf{r p m}\) for \(30\;\mathsf{s}\), further \(100~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) EC solvent was dripped on the surface of precursor film and spin-coated at \(5000\;\mathsf{r p m}\) for \(30~\mathsf{s}\). After that, the \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) precursor film was heated at \(210~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 8 min to obtain black \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}\) films. The \({\approx}50\) nm thickness hole transport layer was prepared by spin-coating the P3HT precursor solution with the concentration of \(10\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) in CB at \(4000\;\mathsf{r p m}\) for \(40\;\mathsf{s}\), followed by annealing at \(120\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 2 min. Finally, Ag layer \((\approx\!100\,\mathsf{n m})\) was thermally evaporated as the top electrode using a shadow mask to form a device with an active area of \(0.04\;\mathsf{c m}^{2}\).
Device Characterization: The absorption spectra and transmission spectra were measured by a UV–vis NIR spectrophotometer (Per-kinElmer, Lambda 950). PL (excitation at \(475\ \mathsf{n m}\)) and TRPL spectra (excitation at \(365\ \mathsf{n m}\)) were measured using an FLS980 spectrometer (Edinburgh Instruments Ltd.) and a PicoQuant FluoQuant 300, respectively. Top-view and cross-sectional SEM images were obtained by field-emission scanning electron microscopy (SEM, ZEISS Gemini SEM 300). The AFM and KPFM
images were obtained using an atomic force microscope from a Park system NX10 equipped with scanning Kelvin probe microscopy. XPS measurements were carried out by a photoelectron spectrometer (Fisher Scientific K-Alpha). DLS measurements were performed by using Zetasizer Nano ZS90 and size distribution by number was obtained by Zetasizer software. Transient photocurrent and photovoltage curves were collected using TranPVC with a pulsed YAG laser. The contact angle was measured using an XG-CAMA1. UPS measurement was performed using PHI 5000 VersaProde III with He I source \((21.22\,\mathsf{e V})\) under an applied negative bias of \(0.9\;\mathrm{V}.\) The \(\mathsf{J}{-}\mathsf{V}\) measurements of PSCs were performed on a Keithley 2400 source under the illumination of an AM 1.5G with \(100\ m\mathsf{W}\ c m^{-2}\) , which was generated by SAN-EIELECTRIC XES-40S2-CE solar simulator, the intensity was calibrated by an NPEL traceable KGS filtered silicon reference cell. The external quantum efficiency (EQE) of PSCs was recorded by using a QTest Station 2000ADISystem (Crowntech Inc.).
DFT Computational Methods: The potential map was calculated by using ATOMISTIX TOOLKIT package (ATK). The first-principles DFT calculations were conducted with the Vienna Ab initio Simulation Package (VASP). The core and valence electronic interactions were described with the frozen-core projector augmented-wave (PAW) potentials. The Kohn–Sham one electron states were expanded in plane waves with a kinetic energy of up to \(500\;\mathrm{eV}.\) The exchange-correlation energy was calculated with the Perdew–Burke–Ernzerhof (PBE) of generalized gradient approximation. Energy convergence of electronic calculations adopted the tolerance of \(10{-}5\;\mathrm{eV}.\) The kinetic energy cutoffs for the plane-wave basis set were optimized to ensure the residual forces on atoms converged to below \(0.01\,{\mathsf{e V}}\,\mathsf{\AA}^{-1}\) .
